/-!
# Invariant separating parameters for a semilinear finite action

Source: de Jong, Families of curves and alterations (1997), Lemma 5.2 proof, p. 613;
Stacks Project, Tags 09I3 and 030W
(blueprint: lem-sfb-invariant-separating-parameters).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v w z

open scoped TensorProduct

namespace Algebra

/-- A finitely generated geometrically integral field extension with compatible finite
semilinear actions admits a full separating family of invariant parameters. The action
homomorphisms need not be injective. The tensor products have their usual commutative
algebra multiplication. Test fields live in a common enlarged universe, so the geometric
integrality test is nonvacuous even when the fields have different universes.

Source: de Jong (1997), Lemma 5.2 proof, p. 613, invariant-parameter choice; the complete
algebraic argument below is the DAG's agent formulation, using Stacks Tags 09I3, 09GH,
0C2Y, 030W, 030Z, 030H and 09HB
(blueprint: lem-sfb-invariant-separating-parameters).

Proof:
1. Put k₀ = k^H and F = E^H. Compatibility gives k₀ ⊆ F. Apply the fixed-field
   theorem to the finite automorphism images of H on k and E separately. These images
   are faithful with the same fixed fields, so k/k₀ and E/F are finite separable.
2. Choose generators a₁, …, aₙ of E/k₀ using finite generation of E/k and finiteness
   of k/k₀. The orbit polynomial of each aᵢ has coefficients in F. Let C be the field
   over k₀ generated by these finitely many coefficients. Each aᵢ is algebraic over C
   and together they generate E/C, which is therefore finite by Tag 09GH.
3. A C-basis of the subspace F ⊆ E is finite and generates F as a field over C.
   Together with the coefficients generating C/k₀ it shows F/k₀ finitely generated.
4. The geometric domain hypothesis implies geometric reducedness of E/k. Since k/k₀
   is separable algebraic, scalar restriction (Tag 0C2Y) makes E/k₀ geometrically reduced.
5. For every L/k₀, tensor the inclusion F ↪ E with the flat k₀-vector space L.
   The resulting ring inclusion F ⊗ L ↪ E ⊗ L has reduced target, hence reduced source.
   Thus F/k₀ is geometrically reduced.
6. Transcendence degree in the towers k₀ ⊆ F ⊆ E and k₀ ⊆ k ⊆ E, with the two
   algebraic extensions from step 1, gives trdeg over k₀ of F equal to d.
7. In positive characteristic use Tag 030W and finite generation to obtain a separating
   transcendence basis of F/k₀. In characteristic zero use Tag 030Z. Step 6 indexes it
   by Fin d, and every parameter is H-fixed because it belongs to F.
8. Put B = k₀(t₁, …, t_d). Separability of F/B and E/F gives separability of E/B
   by Tag 09HB. The finitely many generators from step 2 are algebraic over B, so E/B
   is finite by Tag 09GH.
9. Algebraic base extension k/k₀ preserves algebraic independence of these parameters.
   Put D = k(t₁, …, t_d). A finite B-spanning family also spans E over D. For x ∈ E,
   write its separable B-minimal polynomial P as QR over D, where Q is its D-minimal
   polynomial. From UP + VP' = 1 obtain 1 = Q(UR + VR') + Q'VR. Thus Q is separable,
   proving E/D finite separable, also for the empty family when d = 0.
-/
theorem exists_invariant_separating_parameters
    (k : Type u) (E : Type v) [Field k] [Field E] [Algebra k E]
    (hfg : (⊤ : IntermediateField k E).FG)
    (d : ℕ) (hd : Algebra.trdeg k E = (d : Cardinal))
    (hgeom : ∀ (K : Type (max u v z)) [Field K] [Algebra k K],
      IsDomain (E ⊗[k] K))
    (H : Type w) [Group H] [Finite H]
    (ρk : H →* (k ≃+* k)) (ρE : H →* (E ≃+* E))
    (hcompat : ∀ (h : H) (a : k),
      ρE h (algebraMap k E a) = algebraMap k E (ρk h a)) :
    ∃ t : Fin d → E,
      (∀ (h : H) (i : Fin d), ρE h (t i) = t i) ∧
      AlgebraicIndependent k t ∧
      FiniteDimensional (IntermediateField.adjoin k (Set.range t)) E ∧
      Algebra.IsSeparable (IntermediateField.adjoin k (Set.range t)) E := by
  sorry

end Algebra
